  "narrative_body": "Czechs rallied in 400 municipalities this week to defend Petr Pavel, their president, against his government's attempt to force him to accept a minister he had rejected.\n\nThe demonstrations, organised by Million Moments for Democracy, built on protests in Prague two weeks earlier that drew tens of thousands. Mr Pavel had refused to appoint Filip Turek as a minister after inappropriate social media posts came to light, triggering a constitutional dispute with Petr Macinka, the foreign minister. The breadth of the rallies — reaching hundreds of towns and cities — showed that Czech civil society remains mobilised to defend democratic norms.\n\nEven as protesters backed the president, the governing coalition moved to protect its own leaders from the courts. A parliamentary committee recommended against allowing prosecutors to charge Andrej Babiš, the prime minister, in a fraud case and Tomio Okamura, the speaker, for hate speech. Opposition parties called the decision an attack on the rule of law. The full parliament will vote on March 5.\n\nDespite the domestic tensions, Prague continues to play a significant role in supporting Ukraine. Mr Pavel announced that the Czech-organised ammunition programme has delivered 4.4 million large-calibre rounds to Ukraine, with nearly 2 million delivered in the past year. The initiative, which combines Czech arms dealers with international donor funding from 16 countries, shows how Prague maintains its Ukraine coordination role even under the Babiš government. Mr Pavel also attended the Munich Security Conference, maintaining formal diplomatic engagement.\n\nThe constitutional crisis reflects deeper tensions between democratic institutions and a coalition willing to test their limits. While civil society has mobilised to defend presidential authority, the government shows little hesitation in using its parliamentary majority to shield leaders from accountability. Czech integration with the West — through F-35 commitments, the KHNP nuclear deal, and EU economic dependence — continues to constrain any radical policy shifts, but democratic norms face growing pressure.",
